The Nautilus application development team was working on a git repository /usr/src/kodekloudrepos/beta present on Storage server in Stratos DC. 
However, they reported an issue with the recent commits being pushed to this repo. They have asked the DevOps team to revert repo HEAD to last commit. 
Below are more details about the task:

In /usr/src/kodekloudrepos/beta git repository, revert the latest commit ( HEAD ) to the previous commit (JFYI the previous commit hash should be with initial commit message ).

Use revert beta message (please use all small letters for commit message) for the new revert commit.

SOLUTION
On the Storage server (ststor01)
SSH into the Storage server and switch to root:

bash
ssh natasha@ststor01
sudo su -
Go to the beta repository:

bash
cd /usr/src/kodekloudrepos/beta
Check the current history first (optional but useful):

bash
git log --oneline -3
Revert the latest commit (HEAD) without opening an editor:

bash
git revert --no-commit HEAD
Create the revert commit with the exact required message:

bash
git commit -m "revert beta"
If Git complains about missing user identity, set it first:

bash
git config user.email "devops@example.com"
git config user.name "DevOps"
Then retry the commit.

Verify the revert commit and history:

bash
git log --oneline --decorate -3
git status
You should see a new commit at HEAD with the message:

text
revert beta
The latest changes have been reverted via a new revert commit, and no code was manually edited. No push is required unless explicitly requested.

